  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1155 PM MDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nWeakening low pressure will skirt along our southern border over\nthe next 36 hours. This is already bring an increase in clouds,\nbut the question is will see any rain or thunderstorms. Any real\nthreat for now will be across the southern highlands and central\nmountains starting late this afternoon. Any storms up north\nshould end later this evening, but isolated showers and storms\nare possible overnight...especially across the South Hills and\nAlbion Mountains. We may that area of precipitation shift into\nthe southeast highlands tomorrow, but likely dissipating by\nthen. DO NOT expect any appreciable rainfall with anything that\ndevelops, though. There is a 10-40% chance for gusts over 35 mph\nwith development down south, including outflow winds pushing out\ninto the Magic Valley. Beyond that, expect warm and dry \nconditions to continue at least Friday. Highs will remain in the\n70s and 80s for valley locations, running 10-20 degrees ABOVE \nAVERAGE with the potential of encroaching on daily record highs.\n\nBy the weekend, all models/ensembles/AI models are showing a\nlarge trough of low pressure settling across the West. While the\nidea seems pretty solid, precipitation amounts and how deep this\ndevelops remains up in the air. Our Blend of Models show the\npotential for some appreciable moisture across our area, along\nwith highs dropping back into the 50s by early next week. The\nlower end of Blend forecasts is actually even cooler than that,\nshowing highs in the 40s for some valley locations. Stay tuned.",
